# Documentation & ADRs

Docs are part of the deliverable; keep them truthful and in sync with the code.

## Conventions
- Product requirements today live in `backend/requirments.md` and `frontend/requirments.md`;
  treat them as source-of-truth context for scope, not throwaway notes.
- Cross-cutting architecture notes and ADRs live under `docs/` (create `docs/adr/` when the
  first decision is recorded).
- When a data model doc exists (`docs/data-model.md`, `docs/schema/`), update it in the **same
  change** as the schema/index change it describes.
- Prefer updating an existing doc over creating a new file; never let docs claim something the
  code doesn't do.
- **One decision = one ADR** in `docs/adr/` (Status · Context · Decision · Consequences ·
  Alternatives · Escape hatch). ADRs are immutable once Accepted — supersede with a new one
  rather than rewriting.
- Write any **new architectural decision** (datastore, framework, queue, LLM provider, etc.)
  as an ADR before/with the code that implements it — or ask the user first (`no-assumptions.mdc`).
